format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radiation-meets-matter

objective u3-sort-radiation

statement:
    Tell which common kinds of radiation are ionizing (alpha, beta, gamma and X-rays) and which are not (radio waves, microwaves and visible light).

misconception: If something is called radiation, it must be dangerous.

item u3-l1-choice-xray
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Which of these is ionizing?

options:
    {"id":"radio","label":"Radio waves","note":"Radio waves do not have enough energy to remove electrons."}
    {"id":"xray","label":"X-rays"}
    {"id":"micro","label":"Microwaves","note":"Microwaves do not have enough energy to remove electrons."}
    {"id":"light","label":"Visible light","note":"Visible light does not have enough energy to remove electrons."}

answer: xray

explanation:
    X-rays are ionizing. Radio waves, microwaves and visible light are all on the other side of the line.

item u3-l1-sort-ionizing
type: sort
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

prompt:
    Can it knock electrons out of atoms? Put each one in its group.

buckets:
    {"id":"ionizing","label":"Ionizing","icon":"AqAtomOff"}
    {"id":"non","label":"Not ionizing","icon":"AqAtom"}

items:
    {"id":"xray","label":"X-rays","bucket":"ionizing"}
    {"id":"radio","label":"Radio waves","bucket":"non"}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma rays","bucket":"ionizing"}
    {"id":"light","label":"Visible light","bucket":"non"}

explanation:
    X-rays and gamma rays are ionizing. Radio waves and visible light are not. The ionizing ones carry enough energy to remove electrons.

item u3-l1-tom-light
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics
references: ref-openstax-chem-21-6

statement:
    Visible light is a kind of radiation.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Visible light is radiation too. It is non-ionizing, because it cannot knock electrons out of atoms.

item u3-x-not-ionizing
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Which of these is not ionizing?

options:
    {"id":"beta","label":"Beta particles","note":"Beta particles are ionizing."}
    {"id":"gamma","label":"Gamma rays","note":"Gamma rays are ionizing."}
    {"id":"micro","label":"Microwaves"}

answer: micro

explanation:
    Microwaves are non-ionizing. Beta particles and gamma rays have enough energy to remove electrons from atoms.

item u3-x-pair
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

prompt:
    Which pair is made up of radiation that is not ionizing?

options:
    {"id":"mix","label":"Radio waves and gamma rays","note":"Gamma rays are ionizing, so this pair has one of each."}
    {"id":"pair","label":"Visible light and radio waves"}
    {"id":"ab","label":"Alpha particles and beta particles","note":"Both of these are ionizing."}

answer: pair

explanation:
    Visible light and radio waves are both non-ionizing. The other pairs include at least one kind that can knock electrons out of atoms.

item u3-x-tom-xray-gamma
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-epa-radiation-basics

statement:
    X-rays and gamma rays are both ionizing.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Both are high-energy waves with enough energy to knock electrons out of atoms.
